Let \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) be two different lines in space, and \\(α\\) and \\(β\\) be two different planes in space. Consider the following propositions:  
\\(①\\) If \\(m \parallel α\\) and \\(m \parallel β\\), then \\(α \parallel β\\);  
\\(②\\) If \\(m \parallel α\\) and \\(m \parallel n\\), then \\(n \parallel α\\);  
\\(③\\) If \\(m \perp α\\) and \\(m \parallel β\\), then \\(α \perp β\\);  
\\(④\\) If \\(m \perp α\\) and \\(α \parallel β\\), then \\(m \perp β\\)  
Write down the numbers of all correct propositions \_\_\_\_\_\_.

response:
Given \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are two different lines in space, and \\(α\\) and \\(β\\) are two different planes in space, we know:  
In \\(①\\), if \\(m \parallel α\\) and \\(m \parallel β\\), then \\(α\\) and \\(β\\) either intersect or are parallel, hence proposition \\(①\\) is incorrect;  
In \\(②\\), if \\(m \parallel α\\) and \\(m \parallel n\\), then \\(n \parallel α\\) or \\(n \subset α\\), hence proposition \\(②\\) is incorrect;  
In \\(③\\), if \\(m \perp α\\) and \\(m \parallel β\\), then according to the theorem of perpendicularity between planes, \\(α \perp β\\), hence proposition \\(③\\) is correct;  
In \\(④\\), if \\(m \perp α\\) and \\(α \parallel β\\), then according to the theorem of perpendicularity between a line and a plane, \\(m \perp β\\), hence proposition \\(④\\) is correct.  
Therefore, the answer is: $\boxed{③④}$.  
In \\(①\\), \\(α\\) and \\(β\\) either intersect or are parallel; in \\(②\\), \\(n \parallel α\\) or \\(n \subset α\\); in \\(③\\), according to the theorem of perpendicularity between planes, \\(α \perp β\\); in \\(④\\), according to the theorem of perpendicularity between a line and a plane, \\(m \perp β\\).  
This question examines the judgment of the truth of propositions, the basic knowledge of the spatial relationships between lines, planes, and the ability to reason, solve problems, and imagine spatially. It also tests the ability to reduce and transform concepts and to integrate numerical and geometric thinking, making it a medium-difficulty question.

instruction:
