Given that $A=\{x|3x-7>0\}$, we first solve the inequality for $x$:
\begin{align*}
3x-7 &> 0 \\
3x &> 7 \\
x &> \frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}

So the set $A$ can be written as:
$$
A = \left\{ x | x > \frac{7}{3} \right\}
$$

Now, the complement of $A$ in $\mathbb{R}$, denoted by $\complement_{\mathbb{R}} A$, would consist of all the real numbers that are not in $A$. Therefore, these numbers will satisfy the inequality $x \leq \frac{7}{3}$:
$$
\complement_{\mathbb{R}} A = \left\{ x | x \leq \frac{7}{3} \right\}
$$

Thus, the complement of $A$ in the real numbers is the set of all real numbers less than or equal to $\frac{7}{3}$.

Therefore, the answer is:
$$
\boxed{\complement_{\mathbb{R}} A = \left\{ x | x \leq \frac{7}{3} \right\}}
$$

