Concept learning  
Definition: Several divisions of the same rational number (all not equal to $0$) are called division powers, such as $2\div 2\div 2$, $\left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)$, etc. By analogy with rational number exponentiation, we denote $2\div 2\div 2$ as $2_{3}$, read as "the third quotient of $2$", $\left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)\div \left(-3\right)$ as $\left(-3\right)_{4}$, read as "the fourth quotient of $-3$". Generally, we denote the division of $n$ $a$'s ($a\neq 0$) as $a_{n}$, read as "$a$ to the power of $n$ quotient".  
Preliminary exploration  
$(1)$ Write down the result directly: $2_{3}=$______;  
$(2)$ Regarding division powers, which of the following statements is incorrect?  
① The second quotient of any non-zero number is equal to $1$;  
② For any positive integer $n$, $\left(-1\right)_{n}=-1$;  
③ $3_{4}=4_{3}$;  
④ The result of a negative number raised to an odd power is negative, and the result of a negative number raised to an even power is positive.  
In-depth thinking  
We know that subtraction of rational numbers can be converted into addition, and division can be converted into multiplication. How can division powers of rational numbers be converted into exponentiation? For example: ${2_4}=2\div 2\div 2\div 2=2\times\frac{1}{2}\times\frac{1}{2}\times\frac{1}{2}=({\frac{1}{2}})^2$.  
