The initial midpoint $M$ is \begin{align*}
M = M(m, n) = \left(\frac{a+c}{2}, \frac{b+d}{2}\right).
\end{align*}
Post-movement, the coordinates of $A$ become $(a+5, b+10)$ and those of $B$ become $(c-12, d-5)$. The coordinates of the new midpoint $M'$ are \begin{align*}
M' = \left(\frac{a+5 + c-12}{2}, \frac{b+10 + d-5}{2}\right) &= \left(\frac{a+c}{2} - \frac{7}{2}, \frac{b+d}{2} + \frac{5}{2}\right) \\
&= (m - 3.5, n + 2.5).
\end{align*}
The distance between $M$ and $M'$ is calculated as follows:
\begin{align*}
